  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1218 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nBehind a line of precipitation that went through this morning, the \nprecipitation chances will stay out of central and northeastern SD \nfor the rest of today and tonight. Temperatures today will be warmer \nthan normal for this time of the year, with highs warming into the \nlow 40s to low 50s over northeastern SD and in the the 50s over \ncentral SD.  \n\nThe west to northwesterly winds will be picking up this afternoon \nand Saturday over central SD. Gusts this afternoon will get up \naround 25 to 40 mph over central SD, with the strongest gusts west \nof the Missouri River. While these winds will decrease overnight, \nthey will increase again Saturday, with gusts of 30-45 mph west of \nthe James River Valley. These stronger winds, as well as drier \nvegetation, are increasing the fire weather concerns for central SD \nthis afternoon and Saturday. This is causing high Grassland Fire \nDanger Index values this afternoon and Saturday over central SD. One \nthing keeping the fire weather concerns from increasing is higher \nrelative humidity values, with values above 45% Friday and above 55% \nSaturday. \n\nA shortwave is moving through WY and southwest SD tonight into \nSaturday morning, which is helping precipitation to develop and move \nover southwest SD Saturday morning. Most of the precipitation is \nexpected to be south of the forecast area, though there is a 15-25% \nchance that portions of south central SD could be clipped with some \nrain during the morning. Saturday afternoon and overnight into early \nSunday morning there will be a little bit of instability over \ncentral and northeastern SD that will interact with the northwest \nflow. This will cause some isolated 10-20% chances for rain Saturday \nafternoon and snow flurries Saturday night. QPF from these isolated \nrain/snow is fairly low, which will cause very little to accumulate \nfrom them. There is a 10-30% chance for a trace to around 0.02 \ninches of liquid precipitation, whether in the form of rain or a \ntrace of snow, to accumulate Saturday across central and \nnortheastern SD. \n\nTemperatures will be cooling slightly into Saturday, with highs in \nthe low to mid 40s. These temperatures will cool even more Sunday \ninto Monday, with highs in the 30s to low 40s on both days. A weak \nhigh pressure will move over SD Sunday afternoon into Monday \nmorning, which will help to keep precipitation from developing and \nmoving over central and northeastern SD.",
